          </row>
        </tabular>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1cm">
      <statement>
        <p>Let <m>h(t)=-ae^{-k(t-b)}+c</m>,
 where <m>a</m>,
 <m>b</m>,
 <m>c</m>,
 and <m>k</m>
 are positive constants. Describe <m>h</m>
 as a transformation of the function <m>y=e^{t}</m>.  (How does <m>c</m> transform <m>e^{t}</m>, ..., think shifts and stretches vertically or horizontally. Can use Desmos to help you)</p>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li><p><m>a</m>:</p></li>
            <li><p><m>b</m>:</p></li>
            <li><p><m>c</m>:</p></li>
            <li><p><m>k</m>:</p></li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Let <m>r(t) = a\ln(t-b)+c</m>,
 where <m>a</m>,
 <m>b</m>,
 and <m>c</m>
 are positive constants. Describe <m>r</m>
 as a transformation of the function <m>y=\ln(t)</m>.  (Can use Desmos to help you.)</p>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li><p><m>a</m>:</p></li>
            <li><p><m>b</m>:</p></li>
            <li><p><m>c</m>:</p></li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Data for the height of a tree is given in the table below; time 
 is measured in years and height of the tree is given in feet. At <url href="https://gvsu.edu/s/0yy">gvsu.edu/s/0yy</url>, you can find a Desmos worksheet with this data already input.</p>
        <tabular>
          <row>
            <cell>t</cell>
            <cell>1</cell>
            <cell>2</cell>
            <cell>3</cell>
            <cell>4</cell>
            <cell>5</cell>
            <cell>6</cell>
            <cell>7</cell>
            <cell>8</cell>
            <cell>9</cell>
            <cell>10</cell>
            <cell>11</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>h(t)</cell>
            <cell>6</cell>
            <cell>9.5</cell>
            <cell>13</cell>
            <cell>15</cell>
            <cell>16.5</cell>
            <cell>17.5</cell>
            <cell>18.5</cell>
            <cell>19</cell>
            <cell>19.5</cell>
            <cell>19.7</cell>
            <cell>19.8</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
        <p>Do you think this data is better modeled by a logarithmic function of form  <m>p(t)=a\ln(t-b)+c</m>
 or by an exponential function of form <m>q(t)=me^{-rt}+n</m>.
 Provide reasons based in how the data appears and how you think a tree grows, as well as by experimenting with sliders appropriately in Desmos. (Note: you may need to adjust the upper and lower bounds of several of the sliders in order to match the data well.)</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </activity>

  <activity>
    <title>Activity 3.5.4</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>Solve each of the following equations for the exact value of the unknown variable (<m>k</m>).</p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="6cm">
      <statement>
        <p><m>41 = 50e^{-7k}</m></p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="6cm">
      <statement>
        <p><m>65=34+47e^{-45k}</m></p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="6cm">
      <statement>
        <p><m>7e^{2k-1}+4=32</m></p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="6cm">
      <statement>
        <p><m>\frac{5}{1+2e^{-10k}}=4</m></p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </activity>

  <activity>
    <title>Example: Logs in the <q>wild.</q></title>
    <introduction>
      <p>When a population grows (decays) at a rate proportional to the current population, the population follows the model of exponential growth (decay): <md>P(t)=Ae^{kt}</md> where <m>P(t)</m> is the population at time <m>t</m>, <m>A</m> and <m>k</m> are constants.  If <m>k&gt;0</m> we are talking about growth (<m>P</m> is increasing), if <m>k&lt;0</m> we are talking about decay (<m>P</m> is decreasing).  Many populations grow/decay exponentially; one of the most common is bacteria.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Consider a population of bacteria <m>P(t)</m> where <m>P</m> is measure in millions of cells and <m>t</m> is in hours.  We know <m>P(0)=2.475</m>, and <m>P(10)=4.298</m>.  Find a model of the form <m>P(t)=Ae^{kt}</m> that fits this data by first plugging in <m>P(0)</m> to determine <m>A</m>, and then plugging in <m>P(10)</m> to determine <m>k</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>How many bacteria are there after 20 hours (<m>t=20</m>)?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>How long does it take the population of bacteria to reach 1 billion cells (1000 million cells)?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </activity>

</handout>
